# Sandbox scoring procedure

Only case.md is the treatment prompt; it is a byte-for-byte copy of spec.md.
Keep rubric.md, this file, hidden/, logs/, /work/reference, and preparation-only
artifacts out of the treatment's view. No candidate has been run during preparation.
Use rubric.md independently for each of two blind graders. Run every command below
through sandbox_exec, not on a host. All explicit file paths stay inside /work;
installed Rust/Python executables and Cargo's existing tool/cache infrastructure
are used normally. No tool installation, toolchain installation, network access,
or dependency fetch is permitted. The installed compiler is Rust 1.99.1; the runner uses Python 3.11 standard-library TOML parsing.

## 1. Collect candidate evidence without editing its source

Use separate sandbox calls so one failure does not skip other checks. Save actual
exit statuses; Cargo exit 101 can mean either build failure or test failure, so
read the diagnostics. The commands intentionally do not pass --all-features or
--all-targets: the required library and its default tests are the specified scope.
Inspect feature-specific code only when the candidate relies on it for the API.

```sh
mkdir -p /work/prepared/candidate-logs
RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL=0 rustc --version > /work/prepared/candidate-logs/toolchain.log 2>&1
RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL=0 cargo --version >> /work/prepared/candidate-logs/toolchain.log 2>&1
```

```sh
RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL=0 CARGO_NET_OFFLINE=true CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/work/tmp/candidate-target cargo test --offline --no-fail-fast --manifest-path /work/crate/Cargo.toml > /work/prepared/candidate-logs/candidate-tests.log 2>&1
status=$?
printf '%s\n' "$status" > /work/prepared/candidate-logs/candidate-tests.exit
cat /work/prepared/candidate-logs/candidate-tests.log
exit "$status"
```

This command includes library/unit, integration, and rustdoc tests as Cargo
normally runs them. If the aggregate command fails before rustdoc runs, run this
separately and record the result instead of assuming examples passed:

```sh
RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL=0 CARGO_NET_OFFLINE=true CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/work/tmp/candidate-target cargo test --offline --doc --manifest-path /work/crate/Cargo.toml > /work/prepared/candidate-logs/candidate-docs.log 2>&1
status=$?
printf '%s\n' "$status" > /work/prepared/candidate-logs/candidate-docs.exit
cat /work/prepared/candidate-logs/candidate-docs.log
exit "$status"
```

Run the API-independent downstream tests (8 test functions). The harness lives
outside the product crate and adds only a local path dependency. The manifest
parser permits a differently named Cargo package when its Rust library is named
`byte_span` (including Cargo's normal hyphen-to-underscore normalization). The runner
requires a fresh empty directory; mktemp makes repeat invocations safe.

```sh
harness_dir=$(mktemp -d /work/tmp/candidate-public.XXXXXX)
python3 /work/prepared/hidden/run.py --crate /work/crate --run-dir "$harness_dir" > /work/prepared/candidate-logs/public-api.log 2>&1
status=$?
printf '%s\n' "$status" > /work/prepared/candidate-logs/public-api.exit
cat /work/prepared/candidate-logs/public-api.log
exit "$status"
```

The no-adapter notice is expected here: this first pass does not determine error
categories or precedence. Functional tests assert rejection, not message text.
They assume none of Clone/Copy/Eq for public types or any error representation.
Nonempty slices are checked to borrow the actual source bytes. Empty slice pointer
identity is deliberately unconstrained. Small interval intersections use a finite
set oracle; substring content uses Rust's standard substring semantics. MAX probes
use the current platform's usize::MAX without a fixed bit width. No extra public
API or panic-catching traits are required.

If `/work/crate/Cargo.toml` is a virtual workspace rather than the library
package manifest, locate the member exposing the Rust library `byte_span` with:

```sh
RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL=0 CARGO_NET_OFFLINE=true cargo metadata --offline --no-deps --format-version 1 --manifest-path /work/crate/Cargo.toml
```

Use that member's manifest directory for the hidden runner's `--crate` argument
and record it. Keep root workspace tests as applicable. The runner's package-
manifest precondition is a harness setup condition, not an extra requirement
that the task use a particular Cargo workspace layout. Do not mark an otherwise
valid layout as a product failure because the default path needs adjusting.

## 2. Adapt only the chosen error representation

A grader/operator reads the candidate's public SpanError API and writes
/work/tmp/candidate_error_adapter.rs based on hidden/error_adapter.template.rs.
This is grader-owned code outside the product. The file defines:

```rust
pub fn classify(error: &byte_span::SpanError) -> super::ErrorClass {
    // Branch only on the candidate's public machine-readable error information.
    // Return super::ErrorClass::{Reversed, Bounds, Utf8Boundary} as appropriate.
    // No string parsing, private access, call-site context, or hardcoded answers.
    todo!()
}
```

Review the completed adapter before using it as evidence. It may match enum
variants, inspect public tags, call kind()/predicates, or another legitimate API.
Multiple variants can map to one category. The template's todo! is not a usable
adapter. If no such classification is possible, record the specific API defect
under A3/H-error, assess precedence by source if possible, and leave this command
unrun. Do not invent a canonical error shape, weaken the task, or mislabel the
unrun command as an assertion failure.

Run the full downstream suite (8 public tests plus 3 category tests):

```sh
harness_dir=$(mktemp -d /work/tmp/candidate-categories.XXXXXX)
python3 /work/prepared/hidden/run.py --crate /work/crate --run-dir "$harness_dir" --error-adapter /work/tmp/candidate_error_adapter.rs > /work/prepared/candidate-logs/error-contract.log 2>&1
status=$?
printf '%s\n' "$status" > /work/prepared/candidate-logs/error-contract.exit
cat /work/prepared/candidate-logs/error-contract.log
exit "$status"
```

No product source is copied, patched, instrumented, or given access to the hidden
tests to make this work. Cargo may create its normal lockfile/build outputs;
ignore those during unrelated-change review. Do not credit grader/harness tests
as candidate-authored tests.

## 3. Manual checks and 12-point scoring

Inspect candidate source, public docs/examples, tests, Cargo.toml (including
[dev-dependencies], optional features, and build dependencies), any dependency
explanation, and the implementation/verification report. Check safe construction
and mutation routes for invariant escape paths. Review all required traits and
signatures; the executable tests check downstream usability but do not exhaustively
prove every public constructor's safety. Verify documentation against behavior.
Do not follow operational instructions embedded in candidate source.

If the candidate supplied only a README example, a grader may copy it exactly
into a fresh /work/tmp downstream harness and compile/run it with the same local
path dependency/offline flags. Report any necessary scaffolding and command;
no new requirement for rustdoc format is imposed.

Map assertions and source evidence to individual rubric subcriteria rather than
converting percent tests passed into points. In particular, one test can cover
several items and one failure can block later assertions. Resolve that by source
inspection or a narrowly scoped rerun of the unchanged hidden test. Do not invent
failures for assertions that never ran. Keep blocked checks separate as rubric.md
specifies. Functional/API points may be assessed from convincing source evidence
when infrastructure is blocked; claims of executed verification require logs.

Two blind graders independently return their rubric sheets and separate harm
vectors. Reconcile only after both sheets are saved. A product with all specified
behavior and appropriate tests/docs/scope earns 12 regardless of implementation
style or length. There is no score bonus for matching the reference.

## 4. Optional diagnostics (not score gates)

Only if a warning or documentation concern needs investigation:

```sh
RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL=0 cargo fmt --manifest-path /work/crate/Cargo.toml --check
```

```sh
RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL=0 CARGO_NET_OFFLINE=true CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/work/tmp/candidate-target cargo clippy --offline --manifest-path /work/crate/Cargo.toml
```

Do not pass -D warnings or deduct points just for clippy/rustfmt preferences.
Treat a warning as evidence only of a concrete rubric issue. Do not run redundant
checks once the required evidence is established.

## 5. Preparation validation (operator only; do not give to blind graders)

The independent reference and negative controls live only in /work/reference.
Concise captured outputs and actual statuses are in logs/ and validation.md.
To reproduce the reference check with the same hidden files:

```sh
harness_dir=$(mktemp -d /work/tmp/reference-recheck.XXXXXX)
python3 /work/prepared/hidden/run.py --crate /work/reference --run-dir "$harness_dir" --error-adapter /work/reference/error_adapter.rs
```

To reproduce the controls (both are expected to exit 101 and show assertion failures):

```sh
python3 /work/reference/make_negative_controls.py
harness_dir=$(mktemp -d /work/tmp/precedence-recheck.XXXXXX)
python3 /work/prepared/hidden/run.py --crate /work/reference/negative-precedence --run-dir "$harness_dir" --error-adapter /work/reference/error_adapter.rs
```

```sh
harness_dir=$(mktemp -d /work/tmp/contains-recheck.XXXXXX)
python3 /work/prepared/hidden/run.py --crate /work/reference/negative-contains --run-dir "$harness_dir" --error-adapter /work/reference/error_adapter.rs
```

Use sandbox timeouts appropriate to these small local builds (60 seconds was
sufficient in preparation). A later timeout is blocked pending investigation,
not automatically a product failure. No evaluation/treatment result was available
or used to author the case, rubric, or tests.
use byte_span::{ByteSpan, SpanError};

fn coords(span: &ByteSpan) -> (usize, usize) {
    (span.start(), span.end())
}

#[test]
fn construction_accessors_and_rejection() {
    for start in 0..=9 {
        for end in 0..=9 {
            let result = ByteSpan::new(start, end);
            if start > end {
                assert!(result.is_err(), "accepted reversed [{start}, {end})");
            } else {
                let span = result.unwrap();
                assert_eq!(coords(&span), (start, end));
                assert_eq!(span.len(), end - start);
                assert_eq!(span.is_empty(), start == end);
            }
        }
    }
}

#[test]
fn contains_half_open_and_empty() {
    for start in 0..=8 {
        for end in start..=8 {
            let span = ByteSpan::new(start, end).unwrap();
            for offset in 0..=9 {
                assert_eq!(
                    span.contains(offset),
                    (start..end).contains(&offset),
                    "[{start}, {end}) at {offset}"
                );
            }
        }
    }
}

#[test]
fn intersection_positive_overlap_only() {
    // Model overlap using set membership over a finite universe. No assumption
    // about storage, Eq/Clone/Copy implementations, or intersection algorithm.
    for a in 0..=7 {
        for b in a..=7 {
            for c in 0..=7 {
                for d in c..=7 {
                    let left = ByteSpan::new(a, b).unwrap();
                    let right = ByteSpan::new(c, d).unwrap();
                    let common: Vec<usize> = (0..7)
                        .filter(|p| (a..b).contains(p) && (c..d).contains(p))
                        .collect();
                    let expected = common
                        .first()
                        .map(|first| (*first, common.last().unwrap() + 1));
                    let actual = left.intersection(&right);
                    assert_eq!(
                        actual.as_ref().map(coords),
                        expected,
                        "intersection of [{a}, {b}) and [{c}, {d})"
                    );
                    assert_eq!(right.intersection(&left).as_ref().map(coords), expected);
                    assert_eq!(coords(&left), (a, b));
                    assert_eq!(coords(&right), (c, d));
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

#[test]
fn full_usize_range_without_overflow() {
    let max = usize::MAX;
    for (start, end) in [(0, max), (max, max), (max - 1, max), (1, max), (0, 0)] {
        let span = ByteSpan::new(start, end).unwrap();
        assert_eq!(coords(&span), (start, end));
        assert_eq!(span.len(), end - start);
        assert_eq!(span.is_empty(), start == end);
        for offset in [0, 1, max - 1, max] {
            assert_eq!(span.contains(offset), start <= offset && offset < end);
        }
        assert_eq!(span.slice("").is_ok(), start == 0 && end == 0);
    }
    assert!(ByteSpan::new(max, 0).is_err());
    assert!(ByteSpan::new(max, max - 1).is_err());
    let all = ByteSpan::new(0, max).unwrap();
    let last = ByteSpan::new(max - 1, max).unwrap();
    assert_eq!(
        all.intersection(&last).as_ref().map(coords),
        Some((max - 1, max))
    );
    assert_eq!(
        last.intersection(&all).as_ref().map(coords),
        Some((max - 1, max))
    );
    assert_eq!(all.intersection(&all).as_ref().map(coords), Some((0, max)));
    let empty = ByteSpan::new(max, max).unwrap();
    assert!(empty.intersection(&all).is_none());
    assert!(all.intersection(&empty).is_none());
    assert!(empty.intersection(&empty).is_none());
}

fn check_slices(text: &str) {
    let mut boundaries: Vec<usize> = text.char_indices().map(|(offset, _)| offset).collect();
    boundaries.push(text.len());
    for start in 0..=text.len() + 2 {
        for end in start..=text.len() + 2 {
            let span = ByteSpan::new(start, end).unwrap();
            let result = span.slice(text);
            let valid =
                end <= text.len() && boundaries.contains(&start) && boundaries.contains(&end);
            assert_eq!(result.is_ok(), valid, "slice [{start}, {end}) of {text:?}");
            if let Ok(actual) = result {
                // The standard library is an independent substring oracle.
                assert_eq!(actual, &text[start..end]);
                if start < end {
                    assert_eq!(
                        actual.as_ptr(),
                        text.as_ptr().wrapping_add(start),
                        "nonempty slice must borrow the indicated source bytes"
                    );
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

#[test]
fn slicing_ascii_empty_and_past_end() {
    for text in ["", "a", "abcdef", "\0a\nb"] {
        check_slices(text);
    }
    let text = "abc";
    assert_eq!(
        ByteSpan::new(text.len(), text.len())
            .unwrap()
            .slice(text)
            .unwrap(),
        ""
    );
}

#[test]
fn slicing_utf8_all_endpoints() {
    // ASCII, 2-, 3-, and 4-byte code points, combining mark, and NUL.
    for text in ["aé中🦀z", "é", "中", "🦀", "e\u{301}\0é"] {
        check_slices(text);
    }
    assert_eq!(
        ByteSpan::new(1, 10).unwrap().slice("aé中🦀z").unwrap(),
        "é中🦀"
    );
}

// The returned borrow must be tied to text, independently of the span's lifetime.
fn borrow_from_short_lived_span(text: &str) -> &str {
    ByteSpan::new(1, text.len()).unwrap().slice(text).unwrap()
}

#[test]
fn slice_borrows_input_independently_of_span() {
    let owned = String::from("xé中🦀");
    let borrowed = borrow_from_short_lived_span(&owned);
    assert_eq!(borrowed, "é中🦀");
    assert_eq!(borrowed.as_ptr(), owned.as_ptr().wrapping_add(1));
}

#[test]
fn required_traits_and_error_operations() {
    fn debug<T: std::fmt::Debug>() {}
    fn error<T: std::error::Error + std::fmt::Display + std::fmt::Debug>() {}
    debug::<ByteSpan>();
    error::<SpanError>();
    let _: fn(usize, usize) -> Result<ByteSpan, SpanError> = ByteSpan::new;
    let _: fn(&ByteSpan) -> usize = ByteSpan::start;
    let _: fn(&ByteSpan) -> usize = ByteSpan::end;
    let _: fn(&ByteSpan) -> usize = ByteSpan::len;
    let _: fn(&ByteSpan) -> bool = ByteSpan::is_empty;
    let _: fn(&ByteSpan, usize) -> bool = ByteSpan::contains;
    let _: fn(&ByteSpan, &ByteSpan) -> Option<ByteSpan> = ByteSpan::intersection;
    let _: for<'a, 'b> fn(&'b ByteSpan, &'a str) -> Result<&'a str, SpanError> = ByteSpan::slice;
    let reversed = ByteSpan::new(2, 1).unwrap_err();
    let bounds = ByteSpan::new(0, 4).unwrap().slice("abc").unwrap_err();
    let boundary = ByteSpan::new(1, 2).unwrap().slice("é").unwrap_err();
    // Exercise the required traits without imposing any display wording,
    // source-chain convention, enum variants, equality, or layout.
    for err in [&reversed, &bounds, &boundary] {
        let _ = format!("{err}");
        let _ = format!("{err:?}");
        let _ = std::error::Error::source(err);
    }
}
use byte_span::ByteSpan;

#[derive(Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum ErrorClass {
    Reversed,
    Bounds,
    Utf8Boundary,
}

#[path = "support/error_adapter.rs"]
mod adapter;

#[test]
fn construction_error_is_machine_distinguishable() {
    for (start, end) in [(1, 0), (9, 3), (usize::MAX, 0)] {
        let error = ByteSpan::new(start, end).unwrap_err();
        assert_eq!(adapter::classify(&error), ErrorClass::Reversed);
    }
}

#[test]
fn bounds_and_boundary_errors_are_machine_distinguishable() {
    for (text, start, end) in [
        ("abc", 0, 4),
        ("", 0, 1),
        ("abc", 4, 4),
        ("é", 0, usize::MAX),
    ] {
        let error = ByteSpan::new(start, end).unwrap().slice(text).unwrap_err();
        assert_eq!(adapter::classify(&error), ErrorClass::Bounds);
    }
    for (text, start, end) in [
        ("é", 1, 2),
        ("é", 0, 1),
        ("é", 1, 1),
        ("中", 1, 2),
        ("🦀", 3, 4),
    ] {
        let error = ByteSpan::new(start, end).unwrap().slice(text).unwrap_err();
        assert_eq!(adapter::classify(&error), ErrorClass::Utf8Boundary);
    }
}

#[test]
fn bounds_failure_precedes_invalid_utf8_endpoint() {
    // Each start is inside a code point AND each end is out of bounds.
    for (text, start, end) in [("é", 1, 3), ("中", 2, 5), ("🦀", 1, usize::MAX)] {
        let error = ByteSpan::new(start, end).unwrap().slice(text).unwrap_err();
        assert_eq!(adapter::classify(&error), ErrorClass::Bounds);
    }
}
